# 0004. vLLM runtime with per-deployment API keys derived by HMAC

- Status: Rejected (2026-11-11), replaced by [0009](0009-vllm-runtime-with-random-encrypted-keys.adr)
- Date: 2026-11-11

Rejected by the owner: per-deployment keys are to be random and stored encrypted (0009).
vLLM as the runtime is kept. This record describes the proposal as it was.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: each model runs in vLLM on a RunPod pod reachable through RunPod's public HTTPS
  proxy, so the runtime needs a credential; storing one per deployment adds secret storage.
- **Proposal**:
  - vLLM's OpenAI-compatible server is the runtime.
  - The per-deployment key is `HMAC-SHA256(PERSONAL_AI_RUNTIME_KEY_SECRET, deployment ID)`,
    passed to the pod as `VLLM_API_KEY` and recomputed by the server on every call.
  - No runtime credential is stored anywhere.
- **Expected consequences**: nothing to leak from the database; rotating the one server
  secret invalidates every runtime key at once, but requires restarting every running
  deployment. vLLM's key covers `/v1` only; other vLLM routes stay open on the proxy
  (later SEC-004).
- **Why rejected**: keys are deterministic (anyone with the server secret and an ID has the
  key) and can't be rotated one deployment at a time.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: vLLM, HMAC-SHA256.
- Alternatives: random key per deployment stored in PostgreSQL (became 0009); no key,
  relying on an unguessable proxy URL: anyone with the URL could use the GPU.
